{
  "title": "WorkArena: How Capable are Web Agents at Solving Common Knowledge Work Tasks?",
  "authors": [
    "Alexandre Drouin",
    "Maxime Gasse",
    "Massimo Caccia",
    "Issam H. Laradji",
    "Manuel Del Verme",
    "Tom Marty",
    "L\u00e9o Boisvert",
    "Megh Thakkar",
    "Quentin Cappart",
    "David Vazquez",
    "Nicolas Chapados",
    "Alexandre Lacoste"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2403.07718",
  "abstract": "WorkArena is a benchmark that evaluates the ability of web agents to perform tasks that span the typical daily work of knowledge workers utilizing enterprise software systems. The benchmark consists of 33 tasks and 19,912 unique instances that cover common interactions with the ServiceNow platform, such as filtering lists, filling forms, searching knowledge bases, utilizing service catalogs, reading dashboards, and navigating the workspace via menus. This paper introduces WorkArena and BrowserGym, an environment for designing and evaluating web agents, and presents empirical experiments to assess the performance of state-of-the-art large language model-based agents at solving work-related web tasks.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "WorkArena is a benchmark that tests the ability of web agents to perform tasks that are commonly done by knowledge workers in enterprise software systems. These tasks include filtering lists, filling forms, searching knowledge bases, and navigating menus. <strong>Web agents</strong> are automated assistants that can interact with software through web browsers, making it easier for people to use complex software systems.",
